 "docs": {
  "en": {
   "title": "Telluride first: where to ski in the US West this weekend (12 November)",
   "description": "Height changes everything at Telluride: our model gives 0 in (0 cm) at the base (8,734 ft / 2,662 m), 2 in (6 cm) at mid-mountain (10,620 ft / …",
   "lede": "Height changes everything at Telluride: our model gives 0 in (0 cm) at the base (8,734 ft / 2,662 m), 2 in (6 cm) at mid-mountain (10,620 ft / 3,237 m) and 38 in (96 cm) at the top (12,507 ft / 3,812 m). We weighed the snow already on the ground, the new snow of the last 72 hours and what the forecast adds, and picked Telluride, Cimarron Mountain Club and Wolf Creek. The deepest snowpack of this group is at Telluride, about 42 in (106 cm) on the upper slopes; next are Cimarron Mountain Club 39 in (98 cm) and Wolf Creek 35 in (88 cm). Snow in the mountains is water held back for spring: what falls now feeds rivers and reservoirs later.",
   "sections": [
    {
     "h": "The next days in the forecast",
     "p": [
      "By 15 November the forecast adds 5 in (13 cm) at Telluride, 3 in (7 cm) at Cimarron Mountain Club and 4 in (11 cm) at Wolf Creek to the snowpack.",
      "Forecasts a few days ahead can change: look at the map again the day before you travel."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Where snow stays, where it melts",
     "p": [
      "At Telluride the freezing level reaches 10,039 ft (3,060 m) to 13,123 ft (4,000 m) on 12 November, higher than the top (12,507 ft / 3,812 m): rain or wet snow is possible on all slopes, and some of the snow may turn to runoff.",
      "South-facing slopes take the most sun and melt first; high, north-facing slopes keep their snow the longest."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Where the snow is best",
     "p": [
      "The last clear satellite view of Telluride, on 9 November, shows the slopes covered in snow.",
      "On 9 November, its last clear day, satellites saw snow covering Cimarron Mountain Club.",
      "Our model splits Wolf Creek into three heights: 18 in (46 cm) at the top (11,762 ft / 3,585 m), 14 in (36 cm) at mid-mountain (11,010 ft / 3,356 m), 3 in (8 cm) at the base (10,262 ft / 3,128 m). The last clear satellite view of Wolf Creek, on 3 November, shows the slopes covered in snow."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Staying safe",
     "p": [
      "The avalanche service (Colorado Avalanche Information Center) rates the Telluride area at 1 on the 5-level scale today.",
      "Before you ski off-piste or go touring, read the official avalanche bulletin (links below)."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "advice": [
    "Snow on the map does not mean a resort is open: check lifts and opening dates on the resort's website.",
    "Small ski areas next to the big names often get the same storm and shorter lift lines. Check them on the map first.",
    "Early-season snow is thin. Stay on open, groomed pistes: rocks and grass can hide just under the surface.",
    "Shade protects the snowpack. High, north-facing slopes get less sun, so their snow lasts longer than on the south side.",
    "Get a powder alert in the SnowScope app to know when Telluride gets fresh snow."
   ],
   "captions": {
    "map": "Snow depth in the US West on 12 November 2026 (SnowScope snow model): Telluride, Cimarron Mountain Club, Wolf Creek, Sunlight."
   }
  }
